 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery.** This provides fast updates without a realtime vendor dependency or recurring service fees. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because its repeated reads and delivery lag are less attractive than the accepted connection-management cost; a hosted realtime service was rejected to avoid provider coupling. The project accepts owning socket capacity, reliable publication, replay, and browser reconnect/catch-up behavior.
